\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Data value is a path functional.} (a) Injecting a conflicting block at time $s$ causes damage that tracks cumulative exposure $E(s)$ (blue, fit $1-e^{-cE}$, $R^2{=}0.971$); dropping it at $s$ leaves \emph  {zero} residual regardless of drop time (red)--- erasure is free. (b) Always-on damage is invariant to schedule shape and deterministic update count $K$ (per-seed identical up to one test point, medians bitwise, T1). (c) The first-cause/recency reversal is controlled by the scalar budget $B=K\,E_{\mathrm  {win}}$ alone: GD and SGD at equal $B$ coincide, matching the parameter-free prediction $1-e^{-\bar  {\lambda }_{\mathrm  {path}}B}$ with $\bar  {\lambda }_{\mathrm  {path}}=0.2023$ (T3). Synthetic convex cell, 6 seeds.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:main}{{1}{2}{\textbf {Data value is a path functional.} (a) Injecting a conflicting block at time $s$ causes damage that tracks cumulative exposure $\Expo (s)$ (blue, fit $1-e^{-c\Expo }$, $R^2{=}0.971$); dropping it at $s$ leaves \emph {zero} residual regardless of drop time (red)--- erasure is free. (b) Always-on damage is invariant to schedule shape and deterministic update count $K$ (per-seed identical up to one test point, medians bitwise, T1). (c) The first-cause/recency reversal is controlled by the scalar budget $\B =K\,\Ewin $ alone: GD and SGD at equal $\B $ coincide, matching the parameter-free prediction $1-e^{-\lampath \B }$ with $\lampath =0.2023$ (T3). Synthetic convex cell, 6 seeds}{figure.1}{}}
