\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Hysteresis mechanism on CIFAR-10N (diag20, pilot-19 arm).} \emph  {Top}: the $D$-block is memorized under always-on exposure (loss $\to 0$ by epoch $\approx 90$) and stays memorized after drop@120 (loss $0.06$ vs.\ never $13.6$ at $T$): erasure fails at the representation level, not the decision layer. Inject@late confirms late absorption is fast and partially free (loss only $1.48$ at $T$ after the late exposure window---but test accuracy $0.600$ is still \emph  {below} always-on $0.609$: late injection is worse than never removing). \emph  {Bottom}: gradient conflict $\qopname  \relax o{cos}(g_D,g_A)$ is \emph  {positive} during warm-up ($\ge 0.3$ through epoch $69$) and turns strongly negative only after absorption ($-0.96$ at $T$ for always-on). Hence conflict-based detection cannot fire early enough: at the time the signal exists, the block is already absorbed. Drop@120 relaxes conflict toward $0$ (post-removal the model re-fits $A$), while inject@late shows late exposures are in strong conflict ($-0.63$) yet each such step produces large representation drift---late steps are not cheap. All quantities are medians over 3 seeds; never arm has no $D$, so no $\qopname  \relax o{cos}$.}}{7}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:diag20}{{2}{7}{\textbf {Hysteresis mechanism on CIFAR-10N (diag20, pilot-19 arm).} \emph {Top}: the $D$-block is memorized under always-on exposure (loss $\to 0$ by epoch $\approx 90$) and stays memorized after drop@120 (loss $0.06$ vs.\ never $13.6$ at $T$): erasure fails at the representation level, not the decision layer. Inject@late confirms late absorption is fast and partially free (loss only $1.48$ at $T$ after the late exposure window---but test accuracy $0.600$ is still \emph {below} always-on $0.609$: late injection is worse than never removing). \emph {Bottom}: gradient conflict $\cos (g_D,g_A)$ is \emph {positive} during warm-up ($\ge 0.3$ through epoch $69$) and turns strongly negative only after absorption ($-0.96$ at $T$ for always-on). Hence conflict-based detection cannot fire early enough: at the time the signal exists, the block is already absorbed. Drop@120 relaxes conflict toward $0$ (post-removal the model re-fits $A$), while inject@late shows late exposures are in strong conflict ($-0.63$) yet each such step produces large representation drift---late steps are not cheap. All quantities are medians over 3 seeds; never arm has no $D$, so no $\cos $}{table.2}{}}
